{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"# Wire the model classes through the code-review gate (they are declared and ignored)\n\n**Filed as a bug, RECLASSIFIED AS A STORY on operator direction: this is the epic's wiring work — carrying the three-class configuration through the review process — not a defect repair. The observable symptom below is what proves the wiring is absent.**\n\n## Problem\n\n`code-review.yaml` declares a model CLASS on all five of its LLM-calling steps, and **every one of\nthose declarations is silently ignored at runtime.** The gate runs every pass on the single resolved\n`cfg.model`, which is exactly the one-knob behaviour epic 061c exists to remove.\n\nMEASURED, not inferred. A real `rebar review-code` run with `REBAR_USAGE_LOG` (one JSON record per\nLLM call, written at the runner chokepoint `llm/runner.py:545`) under an INVERTED class config\n(`frontier = bedrock:us.anthropic.claude-opus-4-8`, `standard = anthropic:claude-sonnet-4-6`):\n\n    1 code-review-base                             anthropic:claude-opus-4-8   <- declares frontier\n    2 code-review-docs                             anthropic:claude-opus-4-8\n    3 code-review-tests                            anthropic:claude-opus-4-8\n    4 code-review-deletion-impact                  anthropic:claude-opus-4-8\n    5 code-review-project-review-phase-boundaries  anthropic:claude-opus-4-8\n    6 code-review-api-compat                       anthropic:claude-opus-4-8\n    7 code-review-verify                            anthropic:claude-opus-4-8   <- declares standard\n    8 code-review-coach                             anthropic:claude-opus-4-8   <- declares standard\n    9 code-novelty                                  anthropic:claude-opus-4-8\n\nNine calls, all on the bare default. `verify` has declared `model: standard` since story 172e\nclosed, so **this is a pre-existing defect, not a regression** introduced by 3768.\n\n## Three competing explanations; two eliminated by experiment\n\nThis is recorded because each cheaper probe produced the SAME observation, and only the discriminating\none settles it.\n\n- **H3 — the class table falls back to built-in defaults** (the run is scoped to a snapshot read root\n  via `gate_source.gate_read_root(handle)`, so a config-file class table might not be visible).\n  **ELIMINATED:** `_DEFAULT_MODEL_BY_CLASS['standard'] == 'claude-sonnet-4-6'`, so under H3 `verify`\n  would have run on SONNET. It ran on OPUS.\n- **H2 — the config pointer was never loaded.** **ELIMINATED:** with the same env var,\n  `load_class_slots()` -> `resolve_class('frontier')` returns `bedrock:us.anthropic.claude-opus-4-8`,\n  both with and without `repo_root=` (the form the gate uses).\n- **H1 — the step's `model:` never reaches the resolver. CONFIRMED.** It is the only hypothesis that\n  predicts opus for a step declaring `standard`. Corroborated directly:\n  `resolve_model(cfg, step='frontier')` -> `bedrock:...` while `resolve_model(cfg, step=None)` ->\n  `claude-opus-4-8`, which is precisely what was logged.\n\nAlso ruled out: the running package loads the worktree's own gate file with all five declarations\nintact, and `code_review/shim.py:119 review_code` really does execute the gate via\n`gate_dispatch.produce_code_review_verdict`.\n\n## The lead: an asymmetry with the gate that DOES work\n\nThe plan-review gate honours classes — a live two-provider run showed a 41/1 opus/sonnet split across\nproviders. The dispatch paths differ in a way that is a concrete starting point rather than a guess:\n\n- plan-review (`gate_dispatch.py` ~:146-163) wraps `run_workflow` in **`gate_config(cfg)`**, whose own\n  comment says it \"publishes `cfg` so every op (and the non-step ProductionBatchRunner) reads the SAME\n  resolved config via `resolve_gate_config` instead of re-deriving from env\".\n- code-review (`gate_dispatch.py` ~:414-424) wraps only\n  `gate_source.gate_read_root(handle), review_kernel.collect_contract_violations()` — **no\n  `gate_config(cfg)`**.\n\nWhether that omission is the cause or merely adjacent must be established before fixing; do not assume\nit. `workflow/runs.py:154` does pass `step=ctx.step.get(\"model\")` into `resolve_model`, and\n`code_review/batch_runner.py:60-61,83-84` does resolve `req.model_ladder[0]` and set `step[\"model\"]`,\nso both halves look correct in isolation — the defect is in what actually executes for this gate.\n\n## Approach\n\n### STEP 0 (FIRST, and it is a deliverable in its own right): make the usage log able to attribute a call to a STEP.\n\n`llm/usage_log.py` records `op=` — the PROMPT NAME — and nothing else that identifies the caller. That is why the nine-call log CANNOT locate this: `code-review-base` is the prompt used BOTH by the `base`\nstep AND as the `batch:` finder for `round_a`/`round_b`, so no observed record can be attributed to a\nstep. Diagnosing on top of that would mean guessing which path produced which call, and guessing from\nan instrument that cannot see the distinction is how three earlier probes in this epic produced\nconfident wrong answers.\n\nSo the first change is OBSERVABILITY, not a fix: record the STEP ID (and the resolved model's declared\nCLASS, when the value came from a class token) alongside `op` on every usage record. Properties this\nmust have: it is additive (existing consumers keep working — `summarize()` folds the file for\n`$GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY`), it stays a no-op when `REBAR_USAGE_LOG` is unset, and it makes the CURRENT\ndefect visible as data rather than inference. Re-running the live probe after Step 0 is what turns\n\"nine calls on cfg.model\" into \"step X received model Y\", which names the broken seam directly.\n\nThis also outlives the ticket: every future per-pass or per-provider question in this epic (0d76's\nparity arms, eb6e's cutover, 8625's docs) needs exactly this attribution, and 0d76's own research\nalready assumes \"the REBAR_USAGE_LOG rig records per-call model\" — it records the model but not the\nstep, which is the half that matters for a per-PASS claim.\n\n### Then the wiring\n\nDiagnose first, then fix the narrowest seam. Trace one `base` call from `produce_code_review_verdict`\nto the runner and identify where the declared class is dropped. Fix that seam so a declared class\nreaches `resolve_model`/`resolve_model_string` for BOTH single-shot prompt steps and batch steps.\n\nThis ticket does NOT change any declaration in `code-review.yaml` — 3768 already made those correct,\nand 172e's `verify` declaration is correct too. Only the plumbing changes.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `src/rebar/llm/workflow/gate_dispatch.py` — the code-review dispatch path.\n- `src/rebar/llm/workflow/runs.py` and/or `src/rebar/llm/code_review/batch_runner.py` — wherever the\n  drop is; narrow to the actual seam once traced.\n- `tests/` — the end-to-end runtime guard described below.\n\n## Testing\n\n**The missing test is the point of this ticket, not an afterthought.** 3768 shipped 9 tests and a 6/6\nmutation score and STILL missed this, because every assertion checked either YAML content or\n`resolve_model_string(<token>)` in isolation. Nothing observed the ENGINE PASSING the token to the\nresolver, so no mutation could distinguish \"declaration honoured\" from \"declaration ignored\".\n\n- END-TO-END, offline: execute the code-review gate with a RECORDING runner (a fake that captures the\n  model it is handed per step) and assert each of the five steps receives its declared class. This is\n  deterministic and free; it is strictly stronger than a live probe and it is the guard that would\n  have caught this.\n- the same test must FAIL on today's code — confirm RED before fixing.\n- a mutation that removes the fix must break it (assert the anchor exists AND that the text changed;\n  a mutation that silently no-ops looks identical to a test that cannot detect).\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] an offline end-to-end test executes the code-review gate with a recording runner and asserts\n      that EACH of the five LLM steps (`base`, `round_a`, `round_b`, `verify`, `coach`) receives its\n      DECLARED class, not `cfg.model` — and it is observed RED on the pre-fix code\n- [ ] the same test covers BOTH shapes: a single-shot prompt step (`base`, step-level `model:`) and a\n      batch step (`round_a`, `batch.model_ladder`), because they travel different code paths\n- [ ] a test asserts that under a class table whose values differ from `cfg.model`, NO step resolves\n      to `cfg.model` — the assertion that discriminates \"honoured\" from \"ignored\"\n- [ ] `verify`'s pre-existing `model: standard` (172e) is shown to be honoured by the same test, so\n      this closes 172e's latent evidence gap rather than only 3768's\n- [ ] no declaration in `code-review.yaml` is changed by this ticket\n- [ ] [operator-attested] a real `rebar review-code` run with `REBAR_USAGE_LOG` under a class table\n      whose classes differ from `cfg.model` shows the per-pass model ids matching the declarations,\n      cited from the JSONL in a comment — the run that currently DISPROVES the behaviour\n","ticket_type":"story"}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1785609206622110001,"uuid":"804f1800-ffe6-40ec-ad3a-4d87d9453e74"}